From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jason Eggleston <eggnet@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 19:36:30 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll: Take the in-flight alert when a RtlWaitOnAddress()
 wait times out after being dequeued.

RtlWakeAddressSingle() and RtlWakeAddressAll() dequeue a waiter under the
queue lock and alert it after releasing the lock. A waiter whose wait
times out in between finds itself dequeued but returns STATUS_TIMEOUT.
Its alert then arrives with nobody waiting for it and stays pending, and
the thread's next NtWaitForAlertByThreadId() returns at once, whatever it
waits for: typically a SleepConditionVariableCS() or
SleepConditionVariableSRW() with an infinite timeout on an unrelated
condition variable, which wakes up spuriously.

Windows doesn't leave that alert behind: a waiter that finds itself
claimed by a waker after its timeout waits again for the wake before
returning. Do the same. A dequeued waiter has been woken, so take the
alert and report the wake.

Warhammer 40,000: Space Marine 2 crashes under Wine because of this. Its tcmalloc spinlock back-off polls with zero-timeout
WaitOnAddress() calls, whose timeouts race the releasing
WakeByAddressSingle(), and the game's unlooped
SleepConditionVariableCS(INFINITE) then returns early. On Windows 11 the
same stress program shows no early return in 100,000 waits, against 13%
under Wine.

Backported from https://github.com/jegglest/wine/commit/dee180fa83 and refreshed for
the ReadPointerAcquire() cast in Valve's b2a60dcd145.

https://github.com/ValveSoftware/Proton/issues/8072
---
diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/sync.c b/dlls/ntdll/sync.c
--- a/dlls/ntdll/sync.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/sync.c
@@ -883,6 +883,7 @@
 {
     struct futex_queue *queue = get_futex_queue( addr );
     struct futex_entry entry;
+    BOOL queued = FALSE;
     NTSTATUS ret;
 
     TRACE("addr %p cmp %p size %#Ix timeout %s\n", addr, cmp, size, debugstr_timeout( timeout ));
@@ -915,11 +916,20 @@
     if (ReadPointerAcquire( (void * volatile *)&entry.addr ))
     {
         spin_lock( &queue->lock );
-        if (entry.addr)
+        if ((queued = entry.addr != NULL))
             list_remove( &entry.entry );
         spin_unlock( &queue->lock );
     }
 
+    if (!queued && ret == STATUS_TIMEOUT)
+    {
+        /* A waker removed us as the wait timed out: we have been woken, and the waker alerts us right after
+         * releasing the queue lock. Take that alert, as Windows does, or it would end this thread's next wait
+         * early, whatever it waits for. */
+        NtWaitForAlertByThreadId( NULL, NULL );
+        ret = STATUS_SUCCESS;
+    }
+
     TRACE("returning %#lx\n", ret);
 
     if (ret == STATUS_ALERTED) ret = STATUS_SUCCESS;
